#!/usr/bin/env python3
r"""NYXUS Suxyn — the synced-lyric fetcher.                          TRK-3812

Given a track (artist, title, duration) this writes ONE small JSON file into
the user's cache and prints its path. The bar reads that file once per song
and then walks an array; nothing here runs per frame, and nothing here ever
touches the player.

    nyxus-lyrics --artist "Sir Mix-A-Lot" --title "Baby Got Back" --duration 262
    /home/you/.cache/nyxus/lyrics/3f2a....json

⚠ FLAGS, NOT POSITIONALS, AND `--duration` IS IN SECONDS. Three positional
words are silently rejected by argparse, and MPRIS reports `mpris:length` in
MICROseconds — hand it `274000000` and every lookup misses, because duration
is part of both the query and the cache key. Divide by 1e6 first. The shell
does (`Math.round(src.lengthSec)`); a person testing by hand usually does not,
and it has cost two sessions.

── the five constraints this file was written against ────────────────────
1. IT CANNOT AFFECT PLAYBACK. This process only ever READS strings it was
   handed on argv (or, for hand testing, from `playerctl metadata`). It never
   holds the player's D-Bus name, never seeks, never pauses. The shell runs it
   detached and reads the file it names. Worst case is no lyrics.
2. ONE LOOKUP PER TRACK, EVER. The answer — including "there is no answer" —
   is cached on disk under a hash of the normalised artist+title+duration. A
   negative result is cached too, or an obscure track re-queries on every
   play, which is the exact shape of the leak this feature must not be.
3. IT FAILS INVISIBLY. No network, no match, a malformed body, a timeout, a
   DNS failure: the file is written as a MISS, the path is printed, exit 0.
   There is no error path that reaches a surface, and nothing is logged to the
   owner's face. `--debug` prints to stderr for a person debugging by hand and
   is off in every shipped call site.
4. ONLY artist + title + duration LEAVE THIS MACHINE. That is the entire
   payload. No album (LRCLIB accepts `album_name` and it is optional — it is
   deliberately NOT sent), no identity, no listening history, no timestamps of
   what was played when, no MPRIS bus name, no cookies, no persistent id.
   The User-Agent is the fixed string below: urllib's default would announce
   the machine's Python version, which is strictly more than a product name.
   Query cleaning only ever REMOVES text ("(Official Video)" and friends), so
   the cleaned query is a subset of what the page already published.
5. THE EMPTY CASE IS THE COMMON CASE. LRCLIB's catalogue is far smaller than
   YouTube Music's; remixes, live cuts, long mixes and anything obscure return
   nothing. Every code path below is written so that "no words" is a normal,
   quiet, cached outcome, not an error.

── ⚠ WORD TIMINGS ARE USUALLY AN ESTIMATE, AND THE FILE SAYS WHICH ────────
The renderer groups these words into short PHRASES. LRC is timestamped PER
LINE. So each word carries an `exact` flag and there are two routes:

  · EXACT (`exact` = 1) — Enhanced LRC / "A2", where `<mm:ss.xx>` markers sit
    INSIDE the line. When they are there they are used, because they are the
    real thing.
  · ESTIMATED (`exact` = 0) — the common case. The line's stamp and the NEXT
    line's stamp bound an interval, and the words are spread across it
    weighted by syllable count plus a small length term, because "the" and
    "unbelievable" are not sung for the same length of time.

An estimated word time WILL drift audibly on a fast line. This build's owner
listens to hip-hop; dense fast lines are the NORMAL case here, not the edge.
Nothing in this file pretends otherwise, which is why `exact` is per word and
not a property of the document.

── ⚠ SCHEMA 2 · THE FIFTH FIELD IS `anchor`, AND IT IS NOT `exact` ────────
A word is `[start, end, text, exact, anchor]`.

  `exact`   1 when an A2 marker gave this word BOTH its ends.
  `anchor`  1 when this word's START is a LITERAL TIMESTAMP off the file —
            the first word of an LRC line, or the first word after an A2
            marker. Everything else inherits a start computed by the
            syllable spread above.

They are different questions and the second is the one a phrase renderer
needs. On the owner's own test track (Kendrick Lamar, "Not Like Us", 905
words) `exact` is 1 for ZERO words — LRCLIB's record carries no A2 at all —
while `anchor` is 1 for 105 of them. A renderer that starts each phrase on
an anchored word therefore starts on a real stamp far more often than one
that starts on an arbitrary word, and the number is measurable rather than
asserted. See MediaSource.qml § THE PHRASE.

⚠ `anchor` is why SCHEMA moved 1 → 2. A v1 document has four fields and no
way to recover the fifth, and `cached()` already refuses a document whose
`v` does not match, so the bump re-fetches once per track and no migration
code is needed.

── where the LRC parsing came from ───────────────────────────────────────
`parse_lrc_lines()` below is derived from `parse_lrc()` in
`artifacts/api-server/nyxus-scripts/nyxus_media.py` (~line 2037) — same
`[mm:ss.xx]` shape, same sort, same "a line that does not stamp is skipped"
rule. It is a COPY rather than an import because importing that module pulls
in GTK4/GStreamer through `gi.require_version`, which a one-shot cache filler
must not pay for. Two differences, both deliberate, and BOTH are defects in
the original worth reporting rather than silently working around:

  · nyxus_media.py's regex is `\[(\d+):(\d+(?:\.\d+)?)\](.*)` and puts
    EVERYTHING after the first `]` into the text, so an Enhanced-LRC line
    reaches its label as the literal string
    `<00:12.34>I <00:12.61>like <00:12.90>big` — markers and all, rendered
    raw. It does not use A2 timings and it does not strip them.
  · the same regex takes only the FIRST stamp, so an LRC line carrying repeat
    stamps (`[00:12.34][01:44.10] chorus line`) is shown once and missed on
    the repeat.

Both are recorded in CHECKLIST.md against nyxus_media.py's owner. This file
handles both; it does not edit that one.

── audit item 15 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
`nyxus_media.py`'s `find_lrc()` carries the docstring "Local sidecar only. No
network — audit item 15." Item 15 (scripts/audit.sh) bans *fetching CODE*:
`urlretrieve`, live strings naming the retired replit host, and live strings
that are URLs ending in `.py`. Its own comment says so in as many words —
"What is banned is fetching CODE, not touching the network" — and it
deliberately spares `urlopen`, naming `nyxus_weather.py`'s API read as the
precedent. A JSON lyric lookup is data over HTTP, exactly that precedent. So
this file does not violate item 15, and item 15 is not weakened to admit it.
`find_lrc()`'s stricter promise is about THAT function and stays true: it is
untouched and still looks only beside the file.

── the consent gate ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
`lyrics_online` in ~/.config/nyxus/settings.json. Absent or false — the
shipped state — and this script makes no request, writes no cache and prints
nothing. The check is the first thing `main()` does, before any lookup call.
Audit item 45 asserts that ordering from the source, so it cannot be
reordered quietly.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import sys
import time
import unicodedata
from pathlib import Path
from urllib.parse import unquote, urlencode, urlparse

SCHEMA = 2
UA = "nyxus-lyrics/1.0 (+NYXUS Suxyn desktop)"
API = "https://lrclib.net/api"
HTTP_TIMEOUT = 4.0          # seconds, hard. A slow host is a miss.
MISS_TTL = 14 * 24 * 3600   # re-ask about an unknown track once a fortnight
MAX_LINE_SPAN = 12.0        # an "instrumental" gap is not a held word
MAX_WORD_HOLD = 3.0         # nor is the last word of a verse a banner

_DEBUG = False


def dbg(msg: str) -> None:
    if _DEBUG:
        sys.stderr.write("nyxus-lyrics: %s\n" % msg)


# ── the consent gate ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def settings_path() -> Path:
    return Path(os.path.expanduser("~/.config/nyxus/settings.json"))


def consent_enabled() -> bool:
    """True only when the owner has switched `lyrics_online` on himself.

    Every failure mode answers False: no file, bad JSON, wrong type, an
    unreadable home. The default is not "try anyway".
    """
    try:
        doc = json.loads(settings_path().read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except Exception:
        return False
    return doc.get("lyrics_online") is True


# ── the cache ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def cache_dir() -> Path:
    base = os.environ.get("XDG_CACHE_HOME") or os.path.expanduser("~/.cache")
    return Path(base) / "nyxus" / "lyrics"


def norm_key(s: str) -> str:
    """Normalisation for the CACHE KEY ONLY — never for what is sent.

    Case-folded, accent-flattened, punctuation dropped, whitespace collapsed,
    so "Baby Got Back" and "baby  got back!" are one cache entry instead of
    two lookups.
    """
    s = unicodedata.normalize("NFKD", s or "")
    s = "".join(c for c in s if not unicodedata.combining(c))
    s = s.casefold()
    s = re.sub(r"[^\w\s]+", " ", s, flags=re.UNICODE)
    return re.sub(r"\s+", " ", s).strip()


def track_key(artist: str, title: str, duration: int) -> str:
    raw = "%s\n%s\n%d" % (norm_key(artist), norm_key(title), int(duration))
    return hashlib.sha1(raw.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()


# ── query cleaning: it only ever REMOVES ──────────────────────────────────
# YouTube titles carry a lot that no lyric database has ever indexed. Each
# pattern below deletes text; none adds any, so the cleaned query is always a
# subset of what the page itself published.
_JUNK = re.compile(
    r"\s*[\(\[]\s*(?:official\s*)?(?:music\s*)?"
    r"(?:video|audio|lyrics?|lyric\s*video|visualizer|visualiser|hd|hq|"
    r"remaster(?:ed)?(?:\s*\d{4})?|explicit|clean|mv|"
    # audit 2026-09-08: YouTube/MPRIS titles carry these too, and LRCLIB
    # indexes the bare title. "Wanna Be (Extended)" missed every lookup.
    r"extended(?:\s*(?:version|mix|edit))?|remix(?:es)?|live|acoustic|"
    r"slowed(?:\s*(?:\+|and|&|n)\s*reverb)?|sped\s*up|nightcore|"
    r"radio\s*edit|single\s*version|album\s*version|deluxe|bonus\s*track|"
    r"(?:feat|ft|featuring)\.?\s[^\)\]]*)\s*[\)\]]"
    # trailing " - Extended" / " feat. X" without brackets
    r"|\s+-\s+(?:extended|remix|live|acoustic|radio\s*edit|remaster(?:ed)?(?:\s*\d{4})?)\s*$"
    r"|\s+(?:feat|ft)\.?\s.*$",
    re.I,
)
# audit 2026-09-08: "GloRilla & Megan Thee Stallion" never matches LRCLIB's
# artist field; the lead artist does. Used for the last-resort retry.
_ARTIST_SPLIT = re.compile(r"\s*(?:&|,|;|/|\bx\b|\bvs\.?\b|\bfeat\.?\b|\bft\.?\b|\bfeaturing\b|\bwith\b|\band\b)\s+", re.I)
_PAREN = re.compile(r"\s*[\(\[][^\)\]]*[\)\]]")


def lead_artist(artist: str) -> str:
    parts = _ARTIST_SPLIT.split(artist or "", maxsplit=1)
    return parts[0].strip() if parts else artist
_TOPIC = re.compile(r"\s*-\s*topic\s*$", re.I)
_VEVO = re.compile(r"\s*\|\s*vevo\s*$", re.I)


# ⚠ ON CHROMIUM THE ARTIST IS THE CHANNEL, NOT THE ARTIST. Measured on his
# machine: MPRIS reports xesam:artist = "inimeg" with the title
# "Lil Yachty - From The D To The A 2 (feat. Tee Grizzley) [prod. Jetsonmade]".
# lrclib can never match that, because the artist it is handed is an uploader's
# handle. That is a large part of why 685 of the 997 documents in his cache are
# misses.
#
# So when the TITLE already carries "Artist - Title" and the reported artist
# does not appear in it, the title is trusted and split. This is query
# cleaning, not a new data source: no new endpoint, nothing extra is sent, and
# the consent gate this file is built around is untouched.
_SPLIT = re.compile(r"^\s*(?P<a>.{2,60}?)\s+[-–—]\s+(?P<t>.{1,120})\s*$")


def split_channel_title(artist: str, title: str):
    """Return (artist, title), splitting "Artist - Title" when it helps.

    Only fires when the reported artist is NOT already present in the title —
    a track legitimately named "Something - Reprise" by an artist the title
    names is left exactly as it is.
    """
    a = (artist or "").strip()
    ti = (title or "").strip()
    if not ti:
        return artist, title
    if a and a.lower() in ti.lower():
        return artist, title
    m = _SPLIT.match(ti)
    if not m:
        return artist, title
    return m.group("a").strip(), m.group("t").strip()


def clean_title(title: str) -> str:
    t = _JUNK.sub("", title or "")
    t = re.sub(r"\s{2,}", " ", t)
    return t.strip(" -–—\t")


def clean_artist(artist: str) -> str:
    a = _TOPIC.sub("", artist or "")
    a = _VEVO.sub("", a)
    return a.strip()


# ── LRC parsing ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Derived from nyxus_media.py's parse_lrc() (~line 2037). See the header for
# the two things this one does that the original does not, and why that is
# reported rather than fixed here.
_STAMP = re.compile(r"\[(\d+):(\d+(?:[.:]\d+)?)\]")
_A2 = re.compile(r"<(\d+):(\d+(?:[.:]\d+)?)>")


def _secs(mm: str, ss: str) -> float:
    return int(mm) * 60 + float(ss.replace(":", "."))


def parse_lrc_lines(text: str) -> list[tuple[float, str]]:
    """[(start_seconds, raw_line_text)], sorted, repeat stamps expanded.

    `raw_line_text` still carries any `<mm:ss.xx>` A2 markers — the word
    splitter below is what consumes them, so nothing is thrown away here.
    """
    out: list[tuple[float, str]] = []
    for raw in (text or "").splitlines():
        stamps = list(_STAMP.finditer(raw))
        if not stamps:
            continue
        # Only a run of stamps at the START of the line is a stamp; a `[...]`
        # later in the line is part of the words.
        run_end = 0
        for m in stamps:
            if m.start() != run_end:
                break
            run_end = m.end()
        if run_end == 0:
            continue
        body = raw[run_end:].strip()
        for m in stamps:
            if m.end() > run_end:
                break
            out.append((_secs(m.group(1), m.group(2)), body))
    out.sort(key=lambda p: p[0])
    return out


# ── word timing ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
_VOWELS = re.compile(r"[aeiouy]+", re.I)


def syllables(word: str) -> int:
    """A cheap vowel-group count. Wrong on plenty of words and that is fine —
    it is a WEIGHT, not a claim, and it only has to rank "unbelievable" above
    "the"."""
    w = re.sub(r"[^A-Za-z]+", "", word)
    if not w:
        return 1
    n = len(_VOWELS.findall(w))
    if w.lower().endswith("e") and n > 1:
        n -= 1
    return max(1, n)


def weight(word: str) -> float:
    """Syllables carry the estimate; a small length term separates two words
    the vowel counter reads as equal ("strength" vs "so")."""
    return syllables(word) + 0.2 * len(re.sub(r"\s+", "", word))


def words_for_line(body: str, start: float, end: float
                   ) -> list[tuple[float, float, str, int, int]]:
    """One line → [(start, end, word, exact, anchor)].

    A2 markers win when present. Otherwise the words are spread across
    [start, end) weighted by `weight()`.

    ⚠ `anchor` marks the words whose START came off the file rather than out
    of the spread: the first word of the line, and the first word after each
    A2 marker. It is the only field that says WHERE THE TRUTH IS, and the
    phrase builder in MediaSource.qml groups on it.
    """
    marks = list(_A2.finditer(body))
    out: list[tuple[float, float, str, int, int]] = []

    if marks:
        # EXACT. A marker stamps the text that FOLLOWS it, up to the next
        # marker. Anything before the first marker inherits the line's stamp.
        segs: list[tuple[float, str]] = []
        lead = body[:marks[0].start()].strip()
        if lead:
            segs.append((start, lead))
        for i, m in enumerate(marks):
            nxt = marks[i + 1].start() if i + 1 < len(marks) else len(body)
            txt = body[m.end():nxt].strip()
            if txt:
                segs.append((_secs(m.group(1), m.group(2)), txt))
        for i, (t0, txt) in enumerate(segs):
            t1 = segs[i + 1][0] if i + 1 < len(segs) else end
            for k, w in enumerate(txt.split()):
                # Only the FIRST word of a segment sits on the marker; a
                # segment carrying three words gives the other two the same
                # start, which is a shared stamp and not an anchor each.
                out.append((t0, min(t1, t0 + MAX_WORD_HOLD), w, 1,
                            1 if k == 0 else 0))
        return [t for t in out if t[1] > t[0]]

    # ESTIMATED.
    plain = _A2.sub(" ", body)
    words = [w for w in plain.split() if w]
    if not words:
        return []
    span = max(0.0, end - start)
    if span <= 0:
        return []
    span = min(span, MAX_LINE_SPAN)
    total = sum(weight(w) for w in words) or 1.0
    t = start
    for i, w in enumerate(words):
        dur = span * (weight(w) / total)
        t1 = start + span if i == len(words) - 1 else t + dur
        # The line's own stamp is real; every start after it is arithmetic.
        out.append((t, min(t1, t + MAX_WORD_HOLD), w, 0, 1 if i == 0 else 0))
        t = t1
    return [x for x in out if x[1] > x[0]]


def build_timeline(synced: str, track_len: float
                   ) -> list[tuple[float, float, str, int, int]]:
    lines = parse_lrc_lines(synced)
    words: list[tuple[float, float, str, int, int]] = []
    for i, (t0, body) in enumerate(lines):
        if i + 1 < len(lines):
            t1 = lines[i + 1][0]
        elif track_len and track_len > t0:
            t1 = min(track_len, t0 + MAX_LINE_SPAN)
        else:
            t1 = t0 + MAX_LINE_SPAN
        words.extend(words_for_line(body, t0, t1))
    words.sort(key=lambda w: w[0])
    return words


# ── ⚠ THE SIDECAR, AND THE CRASH THAT PAID FOR IT ─────────── TRK-3978 ──
#
# On 2026-09-08 `shell/MediaSource.qml` grew a `--file` argument, on the
# reasonable belief that this program read the `.lrc` sitting beside a local
# track. IT DID NOT. argparse rejected the unknown flag, printed a usage
# message to a stderr nobody was reading and exited 2 — so NOTHING reached
# stdout, the shell saw no path, and every track on the machine read as "no
# lyrics" for a whole night while the owner said four times that lyrics were
# not showing. Reverting that one file fixed it in a single restart.
#
# Two things come out of that and both are here:
#
#   1. THE FLAG IS REAL NOW. `--file` takes the track's own location (a path
#      or a `file://` URL) and the `.lrc` beside it wins outright: it is
#      exact to the line, it is offline, and 188 of them already sit in his
#      library. NO NEW ENDPOINT AND NO NEW REQUEST — this is a local file
#      read that happens strictly BEFORE the network is considered, so the
#      best case is now that nothing goes on the wire at all.
#
#   2. ⚠ AND AN UNKNOWN FLAG CAN NEVER AGAIN KILL THIS PROGRAM. `main()`
#      uses `parse_known_args`, so a caller ahead of a deployed helper
#      degrades to the lookup it would have done anyway instead of exiting
#      2 with no output. Rule 3 of this file's own header — "IT FAILS
#      INVISIBLY … there is no error path that reaches a surface" — was
#      never true of its argument parser, and that is exactly the gap the
#      night fell through.
def local_path(where: str) -> str:
    """A `--file` argument as an actual path on this machine, or "".

    MPRIS publishes `xesam:url`, which is percent-encoded and may name a
    host that is not this one. Anything that is not a local file is "" —
    a stream has no sidecar and must not be guessed at.
    """
    s = (where or "").strip()
    if not s:
        return ""
    if s.startswith("file://"):
        u = urlparse(s)
        if u.netloc not in ("", "localhost"):
            return ""                    # someone else's disk
        s = unquote(u.path)
    if not s.startswith("/"):
        return ""                        # http(s), data:, a bare title
    return s


def sidecar_lrc(where: str) -> str:
    """The `.lrc` beside a local track, or "".

    ⚠ THE SAME THREE NAMES `nyxus_media.py`'s `find_lrc()` LOOKS FOR, in the
    same order. The Music app and this helper are both reading the owner's
    library at the same moment on the same machine; two different ideas of
    which file is "the" sidecar would show up as two different lyric sheets
    for one song.
    """
    p = local_path(where)
    if not p:
        return ""
    try:
        f = Path(p)
        stem, folder = f.stem, f.parent
        for name in ("%s.lrc" % stem, "%s.LRC" % stem, "lyrics.lrc"):
            cand = folder / name
            if cand.is_file():
                return str(cand)
    except OSError:
        return ""
    return ""


# ── the one place that touches the network ────────────────────────────────
def _http_json(path: str, params: dict) -> object | None:
    """GET one JSON document. Every failure is None; nothing raises out."""
    # Imported here rather than at module scope so that the import itself
    # cannot happen on a run that never reaches consent.
    from urllib.request import Request, urlopen

    url = "%s/%s?%s" % (API, path, urlencode(params))
    dbg("GET %s" % url)
    try:
        req = Request(url, headers={"User-Agent": UA, "Accept": "application/json"})
        with urlopen(req, timeout=HTTP_TIMEOUT) as r:
            if getattr(r, "status", 200) != 200:
                return None
            body = r.read(2 * 1024 * 1024)
        return json.loads(body.decode("utf-8", "replace"))
    except Exception as exc:                       # noqa: BLE001 — see rule 3
        dbg("lookup failed quietly: %r" % (exc,))
        return None


def lookup(artist: str, title: str, duration: int) -> dict | None:
    """Best synced-lyric record for this track, or None.

    ⚠ THE PAYLOAD IS THE WHOLE ARGUMENT LIST. `album_name` is an accepted
    LRCLIB parameter and is deliberately not sent.
    """
    if duration > 0:
        rec = _http_json("get", {"artist_name": artist,
                                 "track_name": title,
                                 "duration": duration})
        if isinstance(rec, dict) and rec.get("syncedLyrics"):
            return rec
        if isinstance(rec, dict) and rec.get("instrumental"):
            return rec

    hits = _http_json("search", {"artist_name": artist, "track_name": title})
    if not isinstance(hits, list):
        return None
    best, best_gap = None, None
    near, near_gap = None, None
    for h in hits:
        if not isinstance(h, dict) or not h.get("syncedLyrics"):
            continue
        gap = abs(float(h.get("duration") or 0) - duration) if duration else 0.0
        if duration and gap > 5.0:
            # audit 2026-09-08: a YouTube upload is rarely the album length
            # (184 s vs 176 s here). Keep the closest synced hit within 15 %
            # or 30 s as a fallback instead of reporting "no lyrics".
            if gap <= max(30.0, 0.15 * duration) and (near_gap is None or gap < near_gap):
                near, near_gap = h, gap
            continue
        if best_gap is None or gap < best_gap:
            best, best_gap = h, gap
    return best or near


# ── the document ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def miss_doc(artist: str, title: str, duration: int, why: str) -> dict:
    return {"v": SCHEMA, "source": "lrclib", "miss": True, "why": why,
            "instrumental": False, "exact": False, "fetched": int(time.time()),
            "track": {"artist": artist, "title": title, "duration": duration},
            "words": []}


def words_json(words: list) -> list:
    """The on-disk projection of the timeline. ⚠ ONE PLACE, and the selftest
    runs it.

    This function exists because of the bug it now prevents. The line it
    replaces was inline in `main()` and read `for (a, b, c, d) in words`;
    schema 2 added the fifth field to `build_timeline()` and that unpacking
    raised on every real fetch. Because this program is built to FAIL
    INVISIBLY — no output, exit 0, "there are no lyrics" — nothing surfaced.
    A silent-failure design does not get to have an untested serialiser.
    """
    return [[round(w[0], 3), round(w[1], 3), w[2], int(w[3]), int(w[4])]
            for w in words]


def write_doc(path: Path, doc: dict) -> None:
    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    tmp = path.with_suffix(".tmp")
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps(doc, ensure_ascii=False), encoding="utf-8")
    os.replace(tmp, path)          # the reader never sees a half-written file


def cached(path: Path) -> dict | None:
    try:
        doc = json.loads(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except Exception:
        return None
    if not isinstance(doc, dict) or doc.get("v") != SCHEMA:
        return None
    if doc.get("miss") and (time.time() - float(doc.get("fetched") or 0)) > MISS_TTL:
        return None                # a fortnight on, LRCLIB may have it now
    return doc


# ── playerctl, for hand testing only ──────────────────────────────────────
def from_playerctl() -> tuple[str, str, int]:
    import subprocess
    def one(fmt: str) -> str:
        try:
            r = subprocess.run(["playerctl", "metadata", "--format", fmt],
                               capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=3)
            return r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 else ""
        except Exception:
            return ""
    artist = one("{{xesam:artist}}")
    title = one("{{xesam:title}}")
    length = one("{{mpris:length}}")
    try:
        dur = int(int(length) / 1_000_000)
    except Exception:
        dur = 0
    return artist, title, dur


# ── the offline self-test the gate runs ───────────────────────────────────
def selftest() -> int:
    """Executes the real timeline builder. No network, no cache, no HOME.

    Audit item 45 runs this: a gate that greps for `MAX_WORD_HOLD` proves
    nothing, and a gate that RUNS the interpolation over a fixture and asserts
    the invariants is the regression test the estimate deserves.
    """
    fails: list[str] = []

    def say(cond: bool, msg: str) -> None:
        if not cond:
            fails.append(msg)

    # 1 · plain LRC → estimated words, monotonic, non-overlapping, in-span
    plain = ("[00:10.00] I like big butts and I cannot lie\n"
             "[00:14.00] You other brothers can't deny\n")
    w = build_timeline(plain, 200.0)
    say(len(w) == 13, "plain LRC yielded %d words, expected 13" % len(w))
    say(all(x[3] == 0 for x in w), "plain LRC produced a word marked exact")
    say(all(w[i][0] <= w[i + 1][0] for i in range(len(w) - 1)),
        "estimated word starts are not monotonic")
    say(all(w[i][1] <= w[i + 1][0] + 1e-6 for i in range(len(w) - 1)),
        "estimated words overlap")
    first = [x for x in w if x[0] < 14.0]
    say(abs(first[0][0] - 10.0) < 1e-6, "first word does not start on its line")
    say(first[-1][1] <= 14.0 + 1e-6, "a line's words run past the next line")

    # 1b · ANCHORS. Exactly one word per line carries a start off the file,
    # and it is the first one. This is the field the phrase builder groups
    # on, so a change that silently anchors every word — or none — has to
    # fail here rather than surface as a phrase that ignores line breaks.
    say(all(len(x) == 5 for x in w), "a word is not a 5-tuple — schema 2 adds `anchor`")
    say(sum(x[4] for x in w) == 2,
        "plain LRC anchored %d words across 2 lines, expected 2" % sum(x[4] for x in w))
    say(w[0][4] == 1 and first[-1][4] == 0,
        "the anchor is not on the first word of each line")
    say(all(abs(x[0] - 10.0) < 1e-6 or abs(x[0] - 14.0) < 1e-6
            for x in w if x[4] == 1),
        "an anchored word does not start on a literal line stamp")

    # 2 · the weighting is NOT uniform — the whole reason it exists
    a = build_timeline("[00:00.00] the unbelievable\n[00:03.00] x\n", 60.0)
    say(len(a) == 3, "weighting fixture yielded %d words, expected 3" % len(a))
    if len(a) == 3:
        d_the = a[0][1] - a[0][0]
        d_unb = a[1][1] - a[1][0]
        say(d_unb > d_the * 1.5,
            "'unbelievable' (%.2fs) is not held meaningfully longer than "
            "'the' (%.2fs) — the weighting is doing nothing" % (d_unb, d_the))

    # 3 · Enhanced LRC (A2) wins and is marked exact
    a2 = "[00:10.00] <00:10.00>I <00:10.50>like <00:11.90>big\n[00:13.00] x\n"
    e = build_timeline(a2, 60.0)
    ex = [x for x in e if x[0] < 13.0]
    say(len(ex) == 3, "A2 line yielded %d words, expected 3" % len(ex))
    say(all(x[3] == 1 for x in ex), "A2 words are not marked exact")
    if len(ex) == 3:
        say(abs(ex[1][0] - 10.5) < 1e-6,
            "A2 word start %.2f ignores its own marker (10.50)" % ex[1][0])
    # Every A2 word here is its segment's only word, so every one is anchored
    # — an A2 line is the case where `exact` and `anchor` DO coincide, and
    # the two flags must not be quietly collapsed into one.
    say(all(x[4] == 1 for x in ex),
        "a one-word A2 segment is not anchored on its own marker")
    a2m = build_timeline("[00:10.00] <00:10.00>I like big <00:11.90>now\n"
                         "[00:13.00] x\n", 60.0)
    seg = [x for x in a2m if x[0] < 13.0]
    say(sum(x[4] for x in seg) == 2,
        "a 2-segment A2 line anchored %d words, expected 2 (one per marker)"
        % sum(x[4] for x in seg))

    # 4 · repeat stamps expand (nyxus_media.py's parse_lrc takes only the first)
    rep = build_timeline("[00:05.00][01:00.00] chorus\n[01:30.00] x\n", 200.0)
    say(len([x for x in rep if abs(x[0] - 5.0) < 1e-6]) == 1
        and len([x for x in rep if abs(x[0] - 60.0) < 1e-6]) == 1,
        "a repeat-stamped line did not expand to both times")

    # 5 · an instrumental gap does not hold a word on screen for a minute
    gap = build_timeline("[00:00.00] one\n[02:00.00] two\n", 200.0)
    say(gap[0][1] - gap[0][0] <= MAX_WORD_HOLD + 1e-6,
        "a word before a 2-minute gap is held for %.1fs" % (gap[0][1] - gap[0][0]))

    # 5b · THE SERIALISER IS RUN. See words_json()'s own header: the field
    # that made this necessary was added to the builder and not to the
    # writer, and the program's own fail-invisibly contract hid it.
    ser = words_json(build_timeline(plain, 200.0))
    say(len(ser) == len(w), "words_json dropped rows: %d of %d" % (len(ser), len(w)))
    say(all(len(r) == 5 for r in ser),
        "a serialised word is not 5 fields — the document and the builder disagree")
    say(all(isinstance(r[3], int) and isinstance(r[4], int) for r in ser),
        "the flags did not serialise as integers")
    say(json.dumps({"v": SCHEMA, "words": ser}) is not None,
        "the document does not serialise to JSON")

    # 6 · the empty case is quiet, not an exception
    say(build_timeline("", 200.0) == [], "empty lyrics did not yield an empty timeline")
    say(build_timeline("no stamps at all\n", 200.0) == [],
        "unstamped text produced words")

    # 7 · cache keys normalise, and duration is part of the identity
    say(track_key("Sir Mix-A-Lot", "Baby Got Back", 262)
        == track_key("sir mix-a-lot", " Baby  Got Back! ", 262),
        "cache key is not normalisation-stable")
    say(track_key("a", "b", 262) != track_key("a", "b", 263),
        "cache key ignores duration")

    # 8 · cleaning only ever removes
    t = clean_title("Baby Got Back (Official Music Video)")
    say(t == "Baby Got Back", "title cleaning produced %r" % t)
    say(clean_artist("Sir Mix-A-Lot - Topic") == "Sir Mix-A-Lot",
        "artist cleaning did not drop the YouTube ' - Topic' suffix")

    # 9 · ⚠ THE SIDECAR PATH, AND THE FLAG THAT ONCE KILLED THIS PROGRAM.
    #     TRK-3978. `--file` was passed by the shell for a night while this
    #     program had never heard of it; argparse exited 2 and nothing
    #     reached stdout. Both halves of the fix are asserted here: the URL
    #     really decodes to a path, and a `.lrc` beside a file really becomes
    #     words. Neither assertion can pass without the code being present.
    say(local_path("file:///home/x/My%20Song.flac") == "/home/x/My Song.flac",
        "a percent-encoded file:// URL did not decode to a path")
    say(local_path("/home/x/plain.flac") == "/home/x/plain.flac",
        "a bare path was not accepted")
    say(local_path("https://example.com/a.mp3") == "",
        "an http URL was treated as a local file — a stream has no sidecar")
    say(local_path("file://otherbox/home/x/a.flac") == "",
        "a file:// URL naming ANOTHER host was treated as local")
    say(local_path("") == "" and local_path("Some Title") == "",
        "an empty or non-path --file was not refused")

    import tempfile as _tf
    with _tf.TemporaryDirectory() as _td:
        _dir = Path(_td)
        (_dir / "track.flac").write_bytes(b"")
        (_dir / "track.lrc").write_text(plain, encoding="utf-8")
        found = sidecar_lrc(str(_dir / "track.flac"))
        say(found == str(_dir / "track.lrc"),
            "the .lrc beside the track was not found (%r)" % found)
        say(sidecar_lrc("file://" + str(_dir / "track.flac"))
            == str(_dir / "track.lrc"),
            "the sidecar was not found through a file:// URL")
        sw = build_timeline((_dir / "track.lrc").read_text(encoding="utf-8"),
                            200.0)
        say(len(sw) == 13,
            "the sidecar produced %d words where the same LRC over the wire "
            "produces 13 — one parser, two callers" % len(sw))
        (_dir / "bare.flac").write_bytes(b"")
        say(sidecar_lrc(str(_dir / "bare.flac")) == "",
            "a track with no sidecar reported one")

    for f in fails:
        print("FAIL %s" % f)
    print("selftest: %d assertion(s) failed" % len(fails))
    return 1 if fails else 0


# ── main ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    global _DEBUG
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(add_help=True, description=__doc__.split("\n")[0])
    ap.add_argument("--artist", default="")
    ap.add_argument("--title", default="")
    ap.add_argument("--duration", type=float, default=0.0,
                    help="seconds; MPRIS mpris:length / 1e6")
    ap.add_argument("--file", default="",
                    help="the track's own location (path or file:// URL); "
                         "a .lrc beside it is read offline and wins")
    ap.add_argument("--probe", action="store_true",
                    help="print the capability document and exit; no network")
    ap.add_argument("--selftest", action="store_true",
                    help="run the offline timeline assertions (audit item 45)")
    ap.add_argument("--debug", action="store_true")
    # ⚠ `parse_known_args`, NOT `parse_args`, AND IT IS NOT LAZINESS.
    # `parse_args` answers an unknown flag with a usage message on stderr and
    # `SystemExit(2)`. In a program whose entire contract is "print a path or
    # print nothing, exit 0", that is an error path that reaches a surface --
    # as SILENCE, which is the one thing this feature must never be. It cost
    # the owner a night on 2026-09-08 (see § THE SIDECAR). A caller that is
    # ahead of the deployed helper now gets the answer the old helper would
    # have given instead of no answer at all.
    args, extra = ap.parse_known_args(argv)
    _DEBUG = args.debug
    if extra:
        dbg("ignoring unrecognised arguments: %r" % (extra,))

    if args.selftest:
        return selftest()

    if args.probe:
        # Three states, never two — HardwareCaps.qml's rule. This one costs no
        # network: whether lrclib answers is not knowable without asking it,
        # and asking it before consent is the thing we must not do.
        print(json.dumps({
            "v": SCHEMA,
            "enabled": consent_enabled(),
            "settings_file": str(settings_path()),
            "settings_present": settings_path().is_file(),
            "cache_dir": str(cache_dir()),
            "endpoint": "lrclib.net",
            # ⚠ A CAPABILITY, SO A CALLER NEVER HAS TO GUESS AT ONE. The
            # 2026-09-08 outage was a shell that assumed `--file` existed in
            # a helper that had never heard of it. A surface that wants the
            # sidecar can ask for this key first and pass `--file` only when
            # it is true, which makes the deploy order of the two files stop
            # mattering. HardwareCaps.qml's three-state rule, applied to a
            # program instead of to hardware.
            "sidecar": True,
        }))
        return 0

    # ⚠ THE CONSENT GATE. Nothing below this line may run without it, and
    # audit item 45 asserts from the source that no lookup call precedes it.
    if not consent_enabled():
        dbg("lyrics_online is not true — no request, no cache, no output")
        return 0

    artist, title = args.artist, args.title
    duration = int(round(args.duration))
    if not artist and not title:
        artist, title, duration = from_playerctl()

    artist, title = clean_artist(artist), clean_title(title)
    if not title:
        return 0                      # nothing to ask about; say nothing

    path = cache_dir() / ("%s.json" % track_key(artist, title, duration))

    # ⚠ THE SIDECAR IS ASKED BEFORE THE CACHE, AND THAT ORDERING IS THE
    # POINT. A `.lrc` beside his own FLAC is exact to the line, costs no
    # request and is already there for 188 of his tracks. Reading it after
    # the cache would mean a "no match" written a fortnight ago kept hiding a
    # file sitting on the disk the whole time; reading it first also means a
    # stale miss REPAIRS ITSELF the next time the song is played.
    #
    # Nothing here touches the network. `local_path()` refuses anything that
    # is not a file on this machine, so a stream cannot become a lookup.
    side = sidecar_lrc(args.file)
    if side:
        try:
            words = build_timeline(
                Path(side).read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace"),
                float(duration))
        except OSError:
            words = []
        if words:
            dbg("sidecar %s (%d words)" % (side, len(words)))
            write_doc(path, {
                "v": SCHEMA, "source": "sidecar", "miss": False,
                "instrumental": False,
                "exact": any(w[3] == 1 for w in words),
                "fetched": int(time.time()),
                "track": {"artist": artist, "title": title,
                          "duration": duration},
                "words": words_json(words),
            })
            print(path)
            return 0
        # A sidecar that parsed to nothing is not an answer. Fall through and
        # ask the way we always did.

    doc = cached(path)
    if doc is not None:
        dbg("cache hit (%s)" % ("miss" if doc.get("miss") else "%d words" % len(doc["words"])))
        print(path)
        return 0

    rec = lookup(artist, title, duration)

    # ⚠ THE CHANNEL RETRY, AND IT IS A FALLBACK ON PURPOSE.
    # On Chromium the reported artist is the CHANNEL, not the artist: measured
    # here as xesam:artist "inimeg" against the title "Lil Yachty - From The D
    # To The A 2". lrclib can never match that, and it is a large part of why
    # 685 of the 997 documents in this cache are misses.
    #
    # An earlier cut rewrote the query BEFORE asking, and that was wrong: a
    # track legitimately named "Track - Reprise" was split into artist
    # "Track". Asking the real question first and only splitting when the
    # answer is nothing cannot produce that false positive — the worst case is
    # one extra request on a track that was already a miss.
    #
    # No new endpoint and nothing extra is sent about him; this is the same
    # question asked once more, phrased the way the catalogue indexes it.
    if not isinstance(rec, dict):
        alt_a, alt_t = split_channel_title(artist, title)
        if (alt_a, alt_t) != (artist, title):
            dbg("channel retry: %r / %r" % (alt_a, alt_t))
            rec = lookup(alt_a, alt_t, duration)
            if isinstance(rec, dict):
                artist, title = alt_a, alt_t

    if not isinstance(rec, dict):
        # audit 2026-09-08: last resort — lead artist + title with every
        # bracketed suffix removed.
        alt_a, alt_t = lead_artist(artist), _PAREN.sub("", title).strip(" -–—")
        if (alt_a, alt_t) != (artist, title) and alt_a and alt_t:
            dbg("lead-artist retry: %r / %r" % (alt_a, alt_t))
            rec = lookup(alt_a, alt_t, duration)
            if isinstance(rec, dict):
                artist, title = alt_a, alt_t

    if not isinstance(rec, dict):
        write_doc(path, miss_doc(artist, title, duration, "no match"))
        print(path)
        return 0
    if rec.get("instrumental"):
        d = miss_doc(artist, title, duration, "instrumental")
        d["instrumental"] = True
        write_doc(path, d)
        print(path)
        return 0

    words = build_timeline(str(rec.get("syncedLyrics") or ""), float(duration))
    if not words:
        write_doc(path, miss_doc(artist, title, duration, "no synced lines"))
        print(path)
        return 0

    write_doc(path, {
        "v": SCHEMA, "source": "lrclib", "miss": False,
        "instrumental": False,
        "exact": any(w[3] == 1 for w in words),
        "fetched": int(time.time()),
        "track": {"artist": artist, "title": title, "duration": duration},
        "words": words_json(words),
    })
    print(path)
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        sys.exit(0)
    except Exception:                  # noqa: BLE001 — rule 3, to the last line
        sys.exit(0)
